Give up on HTTP servers that stop answering

Three HTTP waits had no bound:

- open_http_stream, which a windowed (streaming) HTTP play opens first,
  used bare reqwest::get: no connect timeout and no limit on the wait
  for response headers.
- The bounded reader feeding a windowed play's decoder awaited the next
  body chunk with no stall timeout.
- DiskCache::revalidate_if_due built a fresh reqwest::Client with no
  timeout and awaited the conditional GET while the cache was locked.

A server that accepts the connection and then sends nothing therefore
hung the load forever. Each such load also held one of the four load
slots, so four of them stopped every later play from loading, and a
stuck revalidation held the cache lock, stopping every load at once.
With carry-over a stuck load no longer blocks its voice's commands, but
it still held its slot and its voice until a stop.

The full-load paths were already bounded (connect 10 s, response 30 s,
60 s without body data). The windowed open now uses the same shared
client and response limit; the bounded reader fails the download with
"Download stalled: no data for 60 seconds" when a chunk does not arrive
in time (back-pressure from the decoder is not a stall); and
revalidate_if_due uses the shared client and gives up after 5 seconds,
the bound DiskCache::revalidate already had, logging "Revalidation
failed for <url>: no response within 5 seconds; keeping cached copy".
The 5 s bound is now one constant used by both.

To test these without waiting tens of seconds, each wait takes its limit
from a private function (open_http_stream_with_response_limit,
into_bounded_reader_with_stall_limit,
revalidate_if_due_with_response_limit) that the public function calls
with the existing constant. src/test_support.rs (unit tests only) adds a
local server that answers fixed bytes and then holds the connection
open, and a capture of the warnings code logs. The paused tokio clock
was not used: with real sockets it can jump past the connect timeout
before a loopback connect is seen.

Still open, recorded in docs/bugs.md: revalidate_if_due holds the cache
lock across its request and does not record a failed attempt, so a
server that never answers costs up to 5 seconds per cached URL on every
freshness tick, with loads queued behind it. docs/features/caching.md
no longer promises "once per interval" for that case.

The design doc's guarantee that plays never wait for each other is
reworded: a play never waits for an earlier play to start, but loads
share four slots, so slow downloads can hold up other loads until they
finish or hit these bounds. commands.md and the CHANGELOG no longer say
"nothing waits".

Tests (each first seen hanging past a 10 s guard with the limits
unused):
- opening_a_stream_gives_up_when_the_server_sends_no_response: "No
  response from server within 0.2 seconds".
- a_bounded_download_reports_a_stall_at_its_limit: the first bytes
  arrive, then "Download stalled: no data for 0.2 seconds".
- revalidation_keeps_the_cached_copy_when_the_server_sends_no_response:
  returns unchanged, keeps the cached copy, and logs the one warning.

Validation: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings,
and a warning-free release build (RUSTFLAGS=-D warnings); fuzz suite 7
passed; broker-backed native suite (MQTTAUDIO_BROKER_TESTS=1, TLS CA
set) 1030 passed, 0 failed, 7 device-gated ignored.

Prompts that led to this work:

- Decision after the design review, asked as "Uncached HTTP plays open
  with no connect, response or stall timeout, and cache revalidation
  runs with no timeout while holding the cache lock, so one dead server
  can wedge loads indefinitely. Fix that here too?" -- "Yes, separate
  commit (Recommended)": use the existing connect, response and stall
  limits for these paths, with tests. Revalidation takes the existing
  5 s revalidation bound rather than the 30 s response limit, because it
  runs with the cache locked.

